# The ONE place tool names live.  Format:  name  [timeout_seconds]  [flags]
#   timeout_seconds: default 30, max 120
#   net:  the sandbox's network namespace (bwrap --share-net). Without it the
#         tool has no network. What a net tool reaches is the core's firewall's call.
#   work: bind the box's persistent /var/lib/sbx-work at /deskbox/work and run
#         there; never together with net.
#   keep: skip the routine reset after this tool; only for tools that run no
#         code they are handed. A failed reset is still retried.
# The console writes a timeout explicitly; `name net` is also accepted.
#
# Never give `net` to a tool that runs code it is handed: an interpreter, a shell,
# a test runner, a build or a package manager. That is code execution plus an
# exit, and this file is where the review that prevents it happens.
#
# Disable a tool by commenting it out:  # off: name [timeout_seconds] [flags]
#
# Add a tool:
#   1. add a line here, e.g. `fetch 30 net` for a tool that needs the network
#   2. write sbx/tools/<name>/run              (runs on the sandbox box)
#   3. write harness/tools/<name>-sbx/tcs.yaml (the contract, on the trusted box)
#   4. python3 sync.py                         (generates the stub, validates everything)
#   5. deploy: this file -> /etc/sbx/tools.list (harness) and /opt/sbx/tools.list (sbx)
greet   10
netcheck 30 net
page    60 net
fetch   30 net
# The box's workspace (/var/lib/sbx-work, at /deskbox/work in the job). bash runs what it is handed, so the box is reset
# after it; the others run only their own code, so `keep` spares a reboot after each read or grep.
bash    120 work
read    20  work keep
write   20  work keep
edit    20  work keep
glob    30  work keep
grep    60  work keep
